\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Computation graphs for VAEs. Computation graph for VAEs, where $p(\boldsymbol  {z}) = \mathcal  {N}(\boldsymbol  {z}|\boldsymbol  {0}, \mathbf  {I})$, $p(\boldsymbol  {x}|\boldsymbol  {z}, \boldsymbol  {\theta }) = \mathcal  {N}(\boldsymbol  {x}|f(\boldsymbol  {z}), \sigma ^2 \mathbf  {I})$, and $q(\boldsymbol  {z}|\boldsymbol  {x}, \boldsymbol  {\phi }) = \mathcal  {N}(\boldsymbol  {z}|\mu (\boldsymbol  {x}), \Sigma (\boldsymbol  {x}))$. Red boxes show sampling operations which are not differentiable. Blue boxes show loss layers (we assume Gaussian likelihoods and priors). (Left) Without the reparameterization trick. (Right) With the reparameterization trick. Gradients can flow from the output loss, back through the decoder and into the encoder.}}{23}{}\protected@file@percent }
